The user wants me to choose the smallest change that should land today, name the files I intend to rewrite, and note any risks. I should not output file blocks yet - that's for the write turn.

Based on my exploration, the clearest small bug is the `showMsg` function clobbering the footer badge. Let me think about the fix:

Current `showMsg`:
```javascript
function showMsg(text) {
    const msg = document.getElementById("last-updated-badge");
    if (msg) {
      msg.textContent = text;
      setTimeout(function () {
        msg.textContent = "Last updated: --";
      }, 3000);
    }
  }
```

The problem: after 3 seconds, it sets the badge to "Last updated: --" instead of the actual last update time.

Fix options:
1. Call `refreshBadge()` after the timeout to restore the real time
2. Cache the original badge text before overwriting, then restore it
3. Use a separate message element (but that would require HTML changes on all pages)

Option 1 is cleanest - just call `refreshBadge()` in the timeout callback. This re-fetches the stats and updates the badge with the real last wake time.

Files to change:
- `site/app.js` - fix the `showMsg` function

Risk: Very low. The change is isolated to one utility function. `refreshBadge()` already exists and is called every 60 seconds. Calling it once more after a message is harmless. The only risk is if `refreshBadge()` fails (network error), but it already has a catch handler that sets "Last updated: unknown", which is better than "--".

This is a genuine bug fix, small, reviewable, and improves UX on every page with copy buttons.

